<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="50159" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ARTH/299/50159.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="ARTH" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ARTH.xml">Art--History</subject><course id="299" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/ARTH/299.xml">Spec Topics in Art History</course></parents><sectionNumber>CS </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Mod &amp; Contemp Native Amer Art</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Modern &amp; Contemporary Native American Art. In this course, we will study the history of Native American art and artists from the late 19th century up through the present day.  We will consider ways in which a modality of persistence and a commitment to Indigenous futurity characterize the history of modern and contemporary Native American art, performance, and film, and we will engage thoughtfully with a growing body of art-historical scholarship that articulates and utilizes Indigenous research methods and knowledge systems.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>316</roomNumber><buildingName>Art and Design Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Smith" firstName="C">Smith, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>